s to decreased sleep  decreased appetite  and  then and then in terms of the  psychological effects we see increased  confidence  increased sex drive increased energy  and decrease in boredom and loneliness  so these effects are directly related  to the subpopulations that  are known to use methamphetamine so  shift workers use methamphetamine  because they have to stay up at night  similarly  people who are unstably housed  often use methamphetamine at night to  stay up to stay safe  gay men use methamphetamine to increase  sex drive and increase uh  pleasurability during sex  and rural and youth communities often  use methamphetamine to alleviate boredom  and to experiment with a stimulant  so it's important to remember that  all behavior is brain  including  behaviors like using stimulants  so this is a motivated behavior because  it's motivated by physiological effects  and methamphetamine has direct effects  on neurons in stimulating sustained  dopamine release  and so behavioral and potential  medication therapies  affect neurotransmission and so we are  trying to target this neurotransmission  with these therapies in hopes that in  turn corresponds with behavioral  modification  so there's a cycle with  substance use and the brain and how the  brain  goes from one phase to the next in the  presence of substances  so  this figure describes that cycle and  what i really want to point out is the  far right side that's highlighted by the  red box and so  the brain cycles through various phases  depending on how substances are used but  it's really that far right side where  the brain is going through withdrawal  specific to methamphetamine we get this  profound dysphoria  and that is what we hope to target with  medications with behavioral therapies  you'll also see that this profound  dysphoria as it's repeated will lead to  neural adaptations the brain will  actually change  and that will in turn  change the response  the other responses and sort of instead  of