n among the poly substance  groups so  among those that use methamphetamine and  opioids together or  methamphetamine and other stimulants  together  we really don't understand  how  those drugs sort of interact and what  treatments might be most effective in  those groups  secondly these  some of these projects the  center-release naltrexone plus  propropion includes an injectable  product so this is the the long-acting  injectable  naltrexone  also known as vivitrol  and the injectable products are really  important because they eliminate  adherence issues so we've seen in other  studies  that  um  we that the  the effect of these drugs are lessened  when we they're relied to take every day  so the injectable products uh alleviate  that problem  so when looking at the merchandising  studies there's been two main studies  both have been done in a very similar  population in san francisco among men  who have sex with men  the one on the left was the first one  and we saw very a smaller trial of uh 30  individuals randomized to active  condition mertazapine 30 milligrams per  day  30 people randomized placebo and we do  see over the 12 weeks of that that trial  a steady decline in the percent of  individuals testing positive for  methamphetamine through urine drug  screening  and there was a very strong signal at  the end of 12 weeks  similarly  in a replication trial that was done the  figure on the right which was done in  the very same population indoor city san  francisco men who have sex with men  um we see a drop over 12 weeks and then  we also see a continued drop  over 24 weeks um same emergency and 30  milligrams a day  these are very important  findings because merchandising is a very  safe medication it's been approved as an  antidepressant and has anti-depressing  properties which is often a comorbid  condition of people with methamphetamine  news disorder  so these are very promising results  next  is the  extended release injectable naltrexone  plus high-dose bupropion this was the  adapt2 trial that was recently published  this was an adaptive trial so it had two  phases but the take-home point here is  that across the two phases there was an  11  difference um in methamphetamine use  among those who were randomized to the  naltrexone high-dose bupropion group  compared to placebo  so here instead of showing percent  positive urine sexually show percent  negative urines and you see a steady  increase  in the percentage of study participants  with methamphetamine negative urine drug  screens over the course of the study  and you see that both in the first phase  after the initial randomization and you  see that as well in the second phase  when non first stage non-responders are  then  re-randomized and have an opportunity to  receive the naltrexone bupropion  combination  you see those individuals also  experience a significant increase in  methamphetamine negative urines  we also see  a fair amount of positive outcomes  um beyond just  methamphetamine reduction  so  we also see a decrease in  methamphetamine craving very significant  decrease across both stages for those  who received the extended release  naltrexone plus bupropion combination  versus placebo we saw a decrease in  depressive symptoms as measured by the  phq-9 and we also see an increase in the  subjective effects of functioning as  measured by the treatment effective  treatment effectiveness assessment  so in summary there have been about 25  years of research in trying to find  effective medications for  methamphetamine use disorder and other  silent use disorders and there are still  no nothing that's fda approved  mertazapine has significant effects  in men who have sex with men which is  particularly impressive  and the effects are in reduction in use  not abstinence  but so far these effects are limited to  a relatively small patient population  and we really need more trials to  confirm that mertazapine can be used  for reducing methamphetamine use beyond  men who have sex with them  the adapt 2 trials very strong trials  with a strong signal for the extended  release of naltrexone plus high-dose  bupropion combination  but one thing to note about this  combination is that it cannot be used  among individuals using opioids because  the extended release naltrexone  would precipitate withdrawal  among anyone using opioids  and so we actually need an alternative  pharmacotherapy for individuals who are  co-using methamphetamine and opioids  now i would be remiss if i didn't  mention the other treatments that have  shown both efficacy and effectiveness in  reducing methamphetamine use among those  with methamphetamine use disorder  there's contingency management which  provides incentives for negative urine  drug screens for methamphetamine this is  actually the most efficacious and  effective treatment that we currently  have better than any medication or any  other behavioral therapy  motivational interviewing and cognitive  behavioral therapy have also shown  promise  there's the 12 steps approach which is  effective it provides patients with  structure and it provides them with  social support which is necessary as  they go through treatment  and lastly there's a harm reduction  approach  which  is effective in reducing mortality in  this in this patient population  so what that involves is methods to use  methamphetamine more safely so if you  have someone that's using  methamphetamine by injecting it  motivating them to use it either orally  or  smoke it  will  will result in  potentially reduced use reduced  frequency as well as reduced risk for  overdose and death  so  where do we go from here  and we really need to start to focus on  the  patients populations that use multiple  substances  either concurrently  or sequentially  use them together  to address the effects of of certain  drugs so here we have  individuals with methamphetamine use  disorder a portion of which use opioids  now they may have  opiate use disorder  but there's a substantial proportion  that probably don't have opiate use  disorder or at least they don't have a  severe enough opioid use disorder to  make them eligible for treatment for  that opioid use disorder now for  moderate opioid use disorder severe  opioid use disorder  those individuals would qualify for  medications such as methadone which is  the full opioid agonist or buprenorphine  which is a partial opioid agonist  those with  mild opioid use disorder or opioid  misuse would not qualify  for  buprenorphine or methadone  and so the next phase of research we're  really focusing  on this co-using population  now there will be an upcoming study  that's sponsored by nida through the  clinical trials network  and this study will be evaluating  injectable buprenorphine versus placebo  for participants with methamphetamine  use disorder and co-occurring opioid use  there may be prescribed opioids or  opioid misuse they may be either  misusing their prescribed opioids or  getting opioids from the street  or they might actually have mild opioid  use disorder so again it would not  qualify them for treatment  but it's still an issue for them they  still use opioids  in addition to their methamphetamine  so for these participants  we will be in uh screening them over 21  day period and looking for two positive  urine drug screens for methamphetamine  we will be looking for opioid use in at  least two out of the last 30 days and  we'll be looking for a negative urine  drug screen for buprenorphine methadone  and opioids on the day of randomization  because we don't want them  to have opioids in their system  at that time  just in case we would precipitate  withdrawal  there's about 250 participants in this  patient population  or in the study rather and they'll be  randomized in a one-to-one ratio  and then any participants  with um moderate to severe opioid use  disorder that are found during screening  will be referred to treatment for that  opioid use disorder so they'll be  referred to either a methadone clinic or  another alpha chain uh provider who can  prescribe them buprenorphine  and one thing to note  about the buprenorphine is that as i've  mentioned before it is a partial opioid  agonist but it's also a kappa antagonist  and that is why we think that it may be  useful for treatment of  methamphetamine use disorder because it  could potentially alleviate the  dysphoria  that's really prevalent and prominent  when  people who are using methamphetamine and  have severe methamphetamine disorder go  into withdrawal  and in fact there's been a few very  preliminary studies who've looked at if  you've been working for methamphetamine  use disorder and have seen a signal  there's been a small study two small  studies in iran  and then there have been some pragmatic  trials that have been using  buprenorphine for opioid use disorder  but have  found  in their secondary secondary analysis  that it also reduced methamphetamine use  for those individuals who were co-using  opioids and methamphetamines at the  start of the trial  so the objectives and outcomes of this  study are to evaluate whether assignment  to 12 weeks of outpatient injectable  buprenorphine compared to  12 weeks of injectable placebo  reduces methamphetamine use as measured  by twice weekly urine drug screens  during the last four weeks of the study  so weeks nine through twelve  and secondarily we are also going to  look to see if injectable buprenorphine  reduces alternate measures of  methamphetamine use  so self-reported self-reported use as  well as use over the whole 12 weeks as  well as measures of opioid use  and measures of methamphetamine and  opioid co-use and i just want to note  that  although we know that the  methamphetamine using population and  those with moderate and severe  methamphetamine use disorder do use  opioids we really have a lack of  understanding of the patterns of that  use there's very little 