s of the study  the california bridge program across 52  hospitals identified over twelve  thousand patients with the opioid  disorder  administered buprenorphine to over seven  thousand of them  uh 5400 also received a prescription for  buprenorphine  and then about 4 800 actually reported  engagement in a outpatient visit with a  medication assistance treatment provider  at 30 days and so  to hit that mark of about 40 percent  we're engaged at 30 days is really  profound since this is a very vulnerable  high risk population  that goes to the ed for treatment but  also goes to the ed because they're  suffering from intense withdrawal  and they also have you know could have  go to the ed because they've had a prior  overdose  another aspect of the bridge is very  important is addressing disparities and  in this paper we looked to see whether  or not  the california bridge  patient population  um for those who are  particularly disadvantaged they have  medicaid insurance they report unstable  housing they report methamphetamine use  whether or not they receive hospitals or  buprenorphine or they receive a  prescription at discharge in the same  the same way that people without those  disparities and we found that the bridge  is actually doing a great job  and people who report unstable housing  are actually more likely than those who  are stably housed to receive hospital  administer buprenorphine during their eb  visit  those with medicaid and methamphetamine  use are equally as likely  as those with others insurance and  who  do not report methamphetamine news  respectively  and the same thing with um receiving a  buprenorphine prescription at discharge  those with medicaid are more likely to  get that prescription compared to those  with other insurance types and we see no  difference  in prescription  at discharge for those with unstable  housing or stable housing those  reporting methamphetamine use versus  those not reporting methamphetamine use  so in closing 