h-dose bupropion this was the  adapt2 trial that was recently published  this was an adaptive trial so it had two  phases but the take-home point here is  that across the two phases there was an  11  difference um in methamphetamine use  among those who were randomized to the  naltrexone high-dose bupropion group  compared to placebo  so here instead of showing percent  positive urine sexually show percent  negative urines and you see a steady  increase  in the percentage of study participants  with methamphetamine negative urine drug  screens over the course of the study  and you see that both in the first phase  after the initial randomization and you  see that as well in the second phase  when non first stage non-responders are  then  re-randomized and have an opportunity to  receive the naltrexone bupropion  combination  you see those individuals also  experience a significant increase in  methamphetamine negative urines  we also see  a fair amount of positive outcomes  um beyond just  methamphetamine reduction  so  we also see a decrease in  methamphetamine craving very significant  decrease across both stages for those  who received the extended release  naltrexone plus bupropion combination  versus placebo we saw a decrease in  depressive symptoms as measured by the  phq-9 and we also see an increase in the  subjective effects of functioning as  measured by the treatment effective  treatment effectiveness assessment  so in summary there have been about 25  years of research in trying to find  effective medications for  methamphetamine use disorder and other  silent use disorders and there are still  no nothing that's fda approved  mertazapine has significant effects  in men who have sex with men which is  particularly impressive  and the effects are in reduction in use  not abstinence  but so far these effects are limited to  a relatively small patient population  and we really need more trials to  confirm that mertazapine can be used  for reducing methamphetamine use beyond  men who have sex with them  the adapt 2 trials very strong trials  with a strong signal for the extended  release of naltrexone plus high-dose  bupropion combination  but one thing to note about this  combination is that it cannot be used  among individuals using opioids because  the extended release naltrexone  would precipitate withdrawal  among anyone using opioids  and so we actually need an alternative  pharmacotherapy for individuals who are  co-using methamphetamine and opioids  now i would be remiss if i didn't  mention the other treatments that have  shown both efficacy and effectiveness in  reducing methamphetamine use among those  with methamphetamine use disorder  there's contingency management which  provides incentives for negative urine  drug screens for methamphetamine this is  actually the most efficacious and  effective treatment that we currently  have better than any medication or any  other behavioral therapy  motivational interviewing and cognitive  behavioral therapy have also shown  promise  there's the 12 steps approach which is  effective it provides patients with  structure and it provides them with  social support which is necessary as  they go through treatment  and lastly there's a harm reduction  approach  which  is effective in reducing mortality in  this in this patient population  so what that involves is methods to use  methamphetamine more safely so if you  have someone that's using  methamphetamine by injecting it  motivating them to use it either orally  or  smoke it  will  will result in  potentially reduced use reduced  frequency as well as reduced risk for  overdose and death  so  where do we go from here  and we really need to start to focus on  the  patients populations that use multiple  substances  either concurrently  or sequentially  use them together  to address the effects of of certain  drugs so here we have  individuals with methamphetamine use  disorder a portion of which use opioids  now they may have  opiate use disorder  but there's a substantial proportion  that probably don't have opiate use  disorder or at least they don't have a  severe enough opioid use disorder to  make them eligible for treatment for  that opioid use disorder now for  moderate opioid use disorder severe  opioid use disorder  those individuals would qualify for  medications such as methadone which is  the full opioid agonist or buprenorphine  which is a partial opioid agonist  those with  mild opioid use disorder or opioid  misuse would not qualify  for  buprenorphine or methadone  and so the next phase of research we're  really focusing  on this co-using population  now there will be an upcoming study  that's sponsored by nida through the  clinical trials network  and this study will be evaluating  injectable buprenorphine versus placebo  for participants with methamphetamine  use disorder and co-occurring opioid use  there may be prescribed opioids or  opioid misuse they may be either  misusing their prescribed opioids or  getting opioids from the street  or they might actually have mild opioid  use disorder so again it would not  qualify them for treatment  but it's still an issue for them they  still use opioids  in addition to their methamphetamine  so for these participants  we will be in uh screening them over 21  day period and looking for two positive  urine drug screens for methamphetamine  we will be looking for opioid use in at  least two out of the last 30 days and  we'll be looking for a negative urine  drug screen for buprenorphine methadone  and opioids on the day of randomization  because we don't want them  to have opioids in their system  at that time  just in case we would precipitate  withdrawal  there's about 250 participants in this  patient population  or in the study rather and they'll be  randomized in a one-to-one ratio  and then any participants  with um moderate to severe opioid use  disorder that are found during screening  will be referred to treatment for that  opioid use disorder so they'll be  referred to either a methadone clinic or  another alpha chain uh provider who can  prescribe them buprenorphine  and one thing to note  about the buprenorphine is that as i've  mentioned before it is a partial opioid  agonist but it's also a kappa antagonist  and that is why we think that it may be  useful for treatment of  methamphetamine use disorder because it  could potentially alleviate the  dysphoria  that's really prevalent and prominent  when  people who are using methamphetamine and  have severe methamphetamine disorder go  into withdrawal  and in fact there's been a few very  preliminary studies who've looked at if  you've been working for methamphetamine  use disorder and have seen a signal  there's been a small study two small  studies in iran  and then there have been some pragmatic  trials that have been using  buprenorphine for opioid use disorder  but have  found  in their secondary secondary analysis  that it also reduced methamphetamine use  for those individuals who were co-using  opioids and methamphetamines at the  start of the trial  so the objectives and outcomes of this  study are to evaluate whether assignment  to 12 weeks of outpatient injectable  buprenorphine compared to  12 weeks of injectable placebo  reduces methamphetamine use as measured  by twice weekly urine drug screens  during the last four weeks of the study  so weeks nine through twelve  and secondarily we are also going to  look to see if injectable buprenorphine  reduces alternate measures of  methamphetamine use  so self-reported self-reported use as  well as use over the whole 12 weeks as  well as measures of opioid use  and measures of methamphetamine and  opioid co-use and i just want to note  that  although we know that the  methamphetamine using population and  those with moderate and severe  methamphetamine use disorder do use  opioids we really have a lack of  understanding of the patterns of that  use there's very little data of an  understanding of how methamphetamine and  opioids are used together  although we have some anecdotal  information from providers and from  people using methamphetamine and opioids  there are no systematic assessments so  this will give us some clarity on that  and then we'll be also exploring some  other effects of methamphetamine and  opioid use in terms of pain and mood  instability and stress  infectious disease incidence and risk  measures of dysfunction and measures of  adverse events  so this study  is a double-blind placebo-controlled  trial so individuals randomize the  active condition will first get a  sublingual  buprenorphine film lead in  and then on day three they will get  injectable placebo 300 milligrams  compared to placebo who group who will  get a  placebo lead in and then they will get  an injectable buprenorphine  injection at day three and then they'll  get injections every four weeks after  that so at weeks five and nine  and then there will also be a follow-up  visit at week 16.  so in terms of translating  these results the results that we  already know and the results that we  will  hopefully the positive results that we  will hopefully see  into practice we have to understand  what that entails and  how to make these evidence  evidence-based treatments successful  in the real world setting and what we  need to make that happen is we need  inner energetic champions  in the field of substance use treatment  who  have  will raise an emphasis on translating  this these medications and these  practices  into their clinics they need to advocate  for investments in training staff and  changing workflows to make these  medications work  and they also have to emphasize the  whole person  so it's not just about the medication  but it's also about incorporating  behavioral therapies  using other people just besides  clinicians so maybe behavioral  specialists or peer recovery coaches  um  and  continued engagement with staff and  really understanding  how these medications can be used  effectively  there also needs to be a concentration  on infrastructure support so we need to  get since methamphetamine and opioids  both affect populations that are have a  high  degree of health disparities we need to  get these individuals  insured  connected to housing and employment  services  so one model that could  give us some  information and and an example of how to  structure our treatments for  methamphetamine use disorder is the  california bridge  i serve as the pi and project director  for the california bridge  which is a  [Music]  a clinical program that is  funded by samsa and other california  state dollars in the state opioid  response and is continuing to be  defunded by by  different different funding mechanisms  at the moment  and basically what it is is a direct  hospital  payment that goes to f to hiring a  substance use navigator the substance  use navigator serves as a liaison  between the patient and the provider so  as patients come in to emergency  departments  at these california hospitals  they are  right the substance use navigator is  trained to understand and recognize  potential signs and symptoms of opioid  use disorder and they can talk to the  patient about starting medication  particularly buprenorphine and then they  also engage with the patient understand  their barriers and challenges to  treatment and connect them to  other services housing employment and  then they follow up with that patient  that three days seven days and 30 days  to see that they're to to try to get  them to connected to outpatient care  if they return to the ed they engage  with them again at that point  and what we've seen is that this  approach for treatment of opioid use  disorder  by  interface by interfacing with patients  who are most severe and most dire and  have the least likelihood of  getting care in the outpatient setting  is very effective  so these data show that over the first  14 months of the study  the california bridge program across 52  hospitals identified over twelve  thousand patients with the opioid  disorder  administered buprenorphine to over seven  thousand of them  uh 5400 also received a prescription for  buprenorphine  and then about 4 800 actually reported  engagement in a outpatient visit with a  medication assistance treatment provider  at 30 days and so  to hit that mark of about 40 percent  we're engaged at 30 days is really  profound since this is a very vulnerable  high risk population  that goes to the ed for treatment but  also goes to the ed because they're  suffering from intense withdrawal  and they also have you know could have  go to the ed because they've had a prior  overdose  another aspect of the bridge is very  important is addressing disparities and  in this paper we looked to see whether  or not  the california bridge  patient population  um for those who are  particularly disadvantaged they have  medicaid insurance they report unstable  housing they report methamphetamine use  whether or not they receive hospitals or  buprenorphine or they receive a  prescription at discharge in the same  the same way that people without those  disparities and we found that the bridge  is actually doing a great job  and people who report unstable housing  are actually more likely than those who  are stably housed to receive hospital  administer buprenorphine during their eb  visit  those with medicaid and methamphetamine  use are equally as likely  as those with others insurance and  who  do not report methamphetamine news  respectively  and the same thing with um receiving a  buprenorphine prescription at discharge  those with medicaid are more likely to  get that prescription compared to those  with other insurance types and we see no  difference  in prescription  at discharge for those with unstable  housing or stable housing those  reporting methamphetamine use versus  those not reporting methamphetamine use  so in closing i'd like to say that  methamphetamine use disorder is linked  with the neural adaptation  of addiction and targets for treatment  and there's no broadly effective  approved medication for me for  methamphetamine use disorder but there  is some promise for mertazapine  as well as the extended release  naltrexone  plus high-dose bupropion combination and  hopefully we will see  some more evidence that buprenorphine  can also be effective in for reducing  methamphetamine use disorder and this  will be particularly important  in the  population that uses methamphetamine and  opioids together  and this raises the question as to  whether it's time for methamphetamine  use disorder treatment to start with  medication and as we see in the  california bridge  that model is particularly successful  because it uses buprenorphine as a  foundation and then also layers on  other interventions such as the use of a  substance use navigator  harm reduction motivational interviewing  to really foster that relationship  between patients and providers and get  them connected to outpatient care  and lastly integrated treatments provide  help to patients and these include  help that focuses on their biology their  behavior and their culture and these are  all important elements to helping people  reach and maintain goals regarding  reducing their methamphetamine use so  that we can minimize comorbidities  minimize overdose risk and ultimately  minimize mortality  i'd like to acknowledge uh several  people  first dr steve choptel who's my mentor  at ucla  provided  slides for this presentation as well as  the lead investigator of the clinical  trials network study that will be  assessing  the effectiveness of  injectable buprenorphine on reduction of  methamphetamine use  likewise my colleagues at the university  of texas southwestern who are also  working with us on that ctn trial  and my colleagues of the california  bridge the four directors  who are instrumental in  allowing us at ucla to lead some of  these research efforts  and implementing some of our proposals  i'd like to thank you for listening to  this presentation
 

